      "thinking": "Let me analyze this fragment carefully.\n\nThis is part 5 of 5 of a certificate — pages 14 and 15 of 15 of an OIML Certificate R 117/2007-A-NL1-22.02 revision 0.\n\nDocument-level info:\n- Number: R 117/2007-A-NL1-22.02, revision 0\n- Project number: 2638810\n- Page total: 15\n- Member state: The Netherlands\n- Scheme: A (from the number format \"R 117/2007-A-NL1-22.02\")\n\nIssuing authority: NMi (Nederlands Meetinstituut) — from the header \"NMi\". No address details given in this fragment. Should I include issuing_authority? The fragment shows \"NMi\" as the header. I can include name: \"NMi\" but omit other fields. Actually the rule says \"If a field is not present in the markdown, omit it.\" The name NMi is present. I'll include name only.\n\nThe recommendation is R 117 (Measuring systems for liquids other than water). The number R 117/2007-A-NL1-22.02 suggests recommendation R 117, edition 2007, scheme A.\n\nThe certificate number format: \"R 117/2007-A-NL1-22.02\" — R 117 is the recommendation, 2007 is the edition year, A is the scheme, NL1-22.02 is the country/national identifier + number.\n\nSo:\n- recommendation: {id: \"R117\", edition: 2007, amendment: null, scheme: \"A\"}\n\nContent on page 14:\n- Part: Calculating/indicating device\n- Producer: Liquid Controls LLC\n- Type: MASTERLOAD.iQ and MASTERLOADx.iQ\n- Documentation folder: TC11730-1\n\nTable 10 General characteristics:\n- Environmental classes: M3 / E2 + E3\n- Accuracy class: 0.5 (convert 0,5 → 0.5)\n- Ambient temperature range: -40 °C / +70 °C → {min: -40, max: 70}, unit °C\n\nSoftware versions table (validated software versions and checksums):\n- GNU/Linux: version 4.9.88-007, CRC 060afc2bc6f029379c15ece991aa6eb8\n- LCR.iQ software part and version: SR1000 v 1.07, SR1000 v 1.08.01, SR1000 v 1.09.01, SR1000 v 1.09.03, SR1000 v 1.11.00 with CRC checksums\n- I/O board software: SR1010 v 1.06, SR1010 v 1.07.01, SR1010 v 1.08.01, SR1010 v 1.08.03, SR1010 v 1.11.00\n- SENSEiQ board software: SR1011 v 1.00.01, SR1011 v 1.01.03, SR1011 v 1.11.00\n\nThis is a matrix table — I should preserve it in matrix_tables[].\n\nWait, interesting — the LCR.iQ software... The device type is MASTERLOAD.iQ and MASTERLOADx.iQ but the software says \"LCR.iQ software part and version\". That's what the source says, so I'll keep it.\n\nPage 15:\n- Part: Gas elimination device\n- Producer: S.A.M.P.I. S.p.A., Liquid controls LLC, Liquid controls Europe.\n- Type: ESG_1 and ESG_2\n- Documentation folder: TC7329-5\n\nNote: Table 11 heading says \"EGS_1 and EGS_2\" (typo in original — the type listed above is ESG_1 and ESG_2, table says EGS_1 and EGS_2). I'll use what's given. Type designations: ESG_1, ESG_2. Hmm, the table title says \"EGS_1 and EGS_2\" — likely OCR/typo. I'll use ESG_1 and ESG_2 from the \"Type:\" field as primary, maybe mention both. Rule 8: don't fabricate. The type field says ESG_1 and ESG_2. Table 11 says EGS_1 and EGS_2. I'll use ESG_1 and ESG_2 as the type designations since that's the explicit \"Type:\" field.\n\nTable 11 General characteristics:\n- Minimum pressure: 0,1 bar(g) → value 0.1, unit \"bar(g)\" or \"bar\" gauge? The value \"bar(g)\" indicates gauge pressure. I'll use unit_symbol \"bar(g)\". Hmm, better: unit_symbol \"bar\" with a note? The schema says value/unit separation. \"bar(g)\" is bar gauge. I'll use unit_symbol: \"bar(g)\" to preserve fidelity. Or \"bar\". Let me think — rule says match canonical Unicode form when possible; bar(g) isn't a standard unitsdb symbol. I'll use \"bar(g)\" as raw symbol. Actually to be safer for unitsdb lookup, maybe \"bar\" is better, but then we lose gauge info. I'll use \"bar(g)\" — the caller looks up unit_symbol in unitsdb and can fall back. Hmm. The instruction: \"0-15%vol → %vol (%vol isn't in unitsdb; use the raw symbol)\". So raw symbols are OK. I'll use \"bar(g)\".\n\n- Maximum pressure: this is messy:\n  - \"- 10; 25 with F and FD filters.\"\n  - \"- 20; 51 with FMP filters.\"\n  - \"Equal to the maximum P(e) max. of the measuring system in which the Special Gas Extractor is used and not above 51 bar(g).\"\n\nHmm, this is confusing. \"- 10; 25 with F and FD filters\" — probably ranges? Like minimum/maximum? Actually might be \"-10 to 25\" bar(g)? Wait but minimum pressure is 0.1 bar(g). Hmm, \"- 10; 25\" could be OCR of something. Perhaps it means \"10; 25\" — maximum pressure 10... no wait. Possibly the original text is:\n\n\"Maximum pressure: \n- 10; 25 with F and FD filters.\n- 20; 51 with FMP filters.\nEqual to the maximum P(e) max. ...\"\n\nHmm, maybe it's \"-10 to 25\" for F/FD filters and \"-20 to 51\" for FMP filters? Actually given it's a gas elimination device, pressure ranges of -10 to +25 bar(g) and -20 to +51 bar(g) seem plausible (vacuum to pressure). The \"Maximum pressure\" label though... Maybe it lists maximum pressure as \"10; 25\" (two options?) Hmm.\n\nActually, thinking about it: these could be paired values: with F and FD filters, P(e)max = 25 bar; with FMP filters P(e)max = 51 bar. And the \"10\" and \"20\" could be... hmm, \"- 10; 25\" maybe \"≤ 25\" no...\n\nAlternatively the OCR mangled \"Pmax = 10\" hmm.\n\nGiven ambiguity, I should preserve this as a matrix_table row or a config_level characteristic. Since values vary by filter configuration, this is a config-level attribute: axis = \"filter type\".\n\nLet me structure:\nconfig_level: [\n  {\n    attribute: \"maximum_pressure\",\n    axis: \"filter_type\",\n    values: [\n      {condition: \"F and FD filters\", value: {min: 10, max: 25}, unit_symbol: \"bar(g)\"},\n      {condition: \"FMP filters\", value: {min: 20, max: 51}, unit_symbol: \"bar(g)\"},\n      {condition: \"measuring system dependent\", value: \"Equal to the maximum P(e) max. of the measuring system in which the Special Gas Extractor is used and not above 51\", unit_symbol: \"bar(g)\"}\n    ]\n  }\n]\n\nHmm, the third one is textual. Per rule, formulas/symbols stay as strings. The sentence is textual, keep