; 25 with F and FD filters.\n• 20; 51 with FMP filters.\n• Equal to the maximum P(e)max of the measuring system in which the Special Gas Extractor is used and not above 51 bar(g).\n\nHmm \"10; 25\" — maybe it's \"-10; 25\" meaning minimum -10, max 25? In many European notations, a semicolon separates min;max. E.g., \"Temperature: -10; +50 °C\" means from -10 to +50. So \"-10; 25\" = from -10 to 25 bar(g), \"-20; 51\" = from -20 to 51 bar(g). But then it's a pressure RANGE under \"Maximum pressure\" label — the original table row might be \"Pressure\" not \"Maximum pressure\", or the label may be OCR of \"Min/Max pressure\". Actually the table row label is \"Maximum pressure\" — maybe the original is \"Pressure limits\" or the row might have been \"Minimum pressure 0,1 bar(g); Maximum pressure ...\".\n\nGiven \"Minimum pressure 0,1 bar(g)\" is its own row, the maximum pressure row giving \"-10; 25\" is odd because -10 < 0.1 minimum. So negative values don't fit with min 0.1. Unless the device can operate at negative gauge pressure (vacuum)? But minimum pressure 0.1 bar(g) says no.\n\nHmm wait — maybe minimum pressure row and max pressure row: 0.1 bar(g) min. And max: \"-10; 25\" hmm doesn't fit.\n\nAlternative: maybe OCR garbled \"1,0 ; 2,5\"? Unlikely.\n\nAnother thought: the numbers could be in bar(g): maximum pressure 10 or 25 with F and FD filters — meaning two filter options each with a max pressure rating? Like F filter rated 10 bar, FD rated 25? And FMP rated 20 or 51?\n\nI can't resolve. I'll record values as strings preserving the source text with the condition, in config_level. That's the most faithful. Actually, let me use matrix_tables? No — config_level seems better. But strings like \"10; 25\" as value... rule 2 says lists of values should be model matrices, but here we don't know what the list means. Preserving as string is safer than fabricating a range.\n\nHmm, but wait — could \"10; 25\" be \"≤25\"? No.\n\nLet me look at actual data: Liquid Controls