at 20 °C → I can split: intended_products: \"Hydrocarbon based oils and fuels\", maximum_viscosity: 20 mPa·s (at 20 °C). Let me record \"maximum_viscosity\" with value 20, unit \"mPa·s\". And maybe intended_products as string. The unit: mPa·s — canonical might be \"mPa·s\". Use raw symbol \"mPa·s\".\n\nNow — should this fragment produce two \"certified parts\"? The fragment is a continuation of a certificate covering multiple parts (a measuring system with components). Pages 14-15 describe two components: calculating/indicating device and gas elimination device. Since this is part 5 of 5, other parts (measuring sensor, pulser, etc.) are in earlier fragments.\n\nGiven the layered schema, I should produce:\n- certificate: number \"R 117/2007-A-NL1-22.02\", revision 0 (the number field includes \"revision 0\" — I'll put number: \"R 117/2007-A-NL1-22.02\" and note revision in revision_history or a revision field). The schema certificate has: number, scheme, project_number, page_total, member_state, date_issued, oiml_issuer_id. Revision isn't a certificate field, but revision_history exists. The revision history says: Revision \"Initial\", date 7 April 2022, changes \"-\". And \"This revision replaces the previous version.\" Hmm, the number says \"revision 0\". The history says Initial, 7 April 2022. So date_issued: 2022-04-07.\n\n- scheme: \"A\" (from number R 117/2007-A-...).\n\n- page_total: 15 (Page 15 of 15).\n\n- member_state: The Netherlands.\n\n- recommendation: R117, edition 2007, scheme \"A\".\n\n- issuing_authority: name \"NMi\". oiml_issuer_id? Not present. omit other fields.\n\n- manufacturers[]: \n  - Liquid Controls LLC (producer of calculating/indicating device MASTERLOAD.iQ/x.iQ)\n  - S.A.M.P.I. S.p.A., Liquid controls LLC, Liquid controls Europe. (producer of gas elimination device)\n\nHmm — for the gas elimination device, producer is listed as \"S.A.M.P.I. S.p.A.\" then \"Liquid controls LLC, Liquid controls Europe.\" — those may be additional producer names (related companies) on the label. I'll record manufacturers entries. Should addresses be included? Not present. Omit.\n\n- components[]: This cert names sub-assemblies:\n  1. role: \"calculating/indicating device\", type_designations: [\"MASTERLOAD.iQ\", \"MASTERLOADx.iQ\"], documentation: TC11730-1\n  2. role: \"gas elimination device\", type_designations: [\"ESG_1\", \"ESG_2\"], documentation: TC7329-5\n\nThe schema components have: role, type_designations[], alternatives (\"OR\"|\"AND\"|null), characteristics{}. Both types are presumably both certified (\"and\" — but not explicitly \"alternatives\"?). MASTERLOAD.iQ AND MASTERLOADx.iQ — \"and\" suggests both; but often it means the type family. I'll set alternatives: \"AND\"? Hmm, \"alternatives\" field is for when cert offers alternatives (\"Transmitter UMC4 OR UMC4-RM\"). Here \"Type: MASTERLOAD.iQ and MASTERLOADx.iQ\" — I think AND is safe. Actually, hmm. For a gas elimination device \"ESG_1 and ESG_2\" — two sizes of the same device, likely alternatives in application. I'll set \"AND\" for the first, \"AND\" for second since \"and\" is written. Or null. Let me use \"AND\" — it reflects the text.\n\nAlso components can have characteristics — I could attach the tables to components. That might be the cleanest: each component's general characteristics as its characteristics{}. But the schema defines characteristics at top-level with type_level, model_level, config_level. Components have \"characteristics{}\" — a dict. Hmm, \"characteristics{}\" for components — probably sub-characteristics. I could put component-level characteristics there as a dict of canonical labels → structured values. That keeps global characteristics clean.\n\nLet me structure:\n\ncomponents: [\n  {\n    role: \"calculating/indicating device\",\n    type_designations: [\"MASTERLOAD.iQ\", \"MASTERLOADx.iQ\"],\n    alternatives: \"AND\",\n    characteristics: {\n      environmental_class: {value: \"M3 / E2 + E3\", unit_symbol: null},\n      accuracy_class: {value: 0.5, unit_symbol: null},\n      ambient_temperature_range: {value: {min: -40, max: 70}, unit_symbol: \"°C\"}\n    }\n  },\n  {\n    role: \"gas elimination device\",\n    type_designations: [\"ESG_1\", \"ESG_2\"],\n    alternatives: \"AND\",\n    characteristics: {\n      minimum_pressure: {value: 0.1, unit_symbol: \"bar(g)\"},\n      maximum_pressure: ... (config-dependent, can't fit simple object)\n      environmental_class: {value: \"M3 / E3\"},\n      ambient_temperature_range: {value: {min: -25, max: 55}, unit_symbol: \"°C\"},\n      product_temperature_range: {value: {min: -10, max: 50}, unit_symbol: \"°C\"},\n      maximum_viscosity: {value: 20, unit_symbol: \"mPa·s\"}\n    }\n  }\n]\n\nBut wait — the component characteristics{} shape: is it structured values too? The schema example: \"components[]: role, type_designations[], alternatives, characteristics{}\". Rule 2 says every characteristic value must be structured. I'll apply structured values there too.\n\nFor the maximum pressure with filter-dependent values, I could use global characteristics.config_level:\n\ncharacteristics: {\n  config_level: [\n    {\n      attribute: \"maximum_pressure\",\n      axis: \"filter_type\",\n      values: [\n        {condition: \"F and FD filters\", value: \"10; 25\", unit_symbol: \"bar(g)\"},\n        {condition: \"FMP filters\", value: \"20; 51\", unit_symbol: \"bar(g)\"},\n        {condition: \"measuring system\", value: \"Equal to the maximum P(e) max. of the measuring system in which the Special Gas Extractor is used and not above 51\", unit_symbol: \"bar(g)\"}\n      ]\n    }\n  ]\n}\n\nHmm, the schema config_level values: `{condition, value}` — and per rule 5 \"Values follow the same structured form: {condition, value, unit_symbol}\". OK so unit_symbol allowed.\n\nBut is it global or component-level? The maximum pressure is specific to the gas elimination device. I could attach to the component's characteristics as a nested structure... The component characteristics{} is a dict, not array of axes. Hmm. Options:\n\na) Put config_level globally with attribute qualified: \"gas_elimination_device_maximum_pressure\". \nb) Keep component characteristics simple and put the config in global config_level.\n\nI'll do (b) but name the attribute clearly, e.g. attribute \"maximum_pressure\" — since this fragment's only config axis is the gas elimination device filters, it's fine. Maybe better: attribute \"maximum_pressure\" and it's understood from context. Actually, to be safe I'll add axis: \"filter type\" and that's descriptive enough.\n\nAlternatively, record matrix_tables for the software version table:\n\nmatrix_tables: [\n  {\n    name: \"Validated software versions and checksums\",\n    columns: [\"software_part\", \"version\", \"crc_checksum\"],\n    rows: [\n      {software_part: \"GNU/Linux\", version: \"4.9.88-007\", crc_checksum: \"060afc2bc6f029379c15ece991aa6eb8\"},\n      {software_part: \"LCR.iQ software\", version: \"SR1000 v 1.07\", crc_checksum: \"b22c8503bfe9924042aa7002cf5777b4\"},\n      {software_part: \"LCR.iQ software\", version: \"SR1000 v 1.08.01\", crc_checksum: \"1d0f9a85ac081149c8448ed9e09ac98e\"},\n      {software_part: \"LCR.iQ software\", version: \"SR1000 v 1.09.01\", crc_checksum: \"c0ffacce4c4cec16018521104a8584f4\"},\n      {software_part: \"LCR.iQ software\", version: \"SR1000 v 1.09.03\", crc_checksum: \"d270eb3bf9077ff78b28280b30e1bdcb\"},\n      {software_part: \"LCR.iQ software\", version: \"SR1000 v 1.11.00\", crc_checksum: \"458753561247b99027a3a3dca9fef4b9\"},\n      {software_part: \"I/O board software\", version: \"SR1010 v 1.06\", crc_checksum: \"0d8d6df0\"},\n      {software_part: \"I/O board software\", version: \"SR1010 v 1.07.01\", crc_checksum: \"1D993269\"},\n      {software_part: \"I/O board software\", version: \"SR1010 v 1.08.01\", crc_checksum: \"ef7c3c68\"},\n      {software_part: \"I/O board software\", version: \"SR1010 v 1.08.03\", crc_checksum: \"01f6bf3d\"},\n      {software_part: \"I/O board software\", version: \"SR1010 v 1.11.00\", crc_checksum: \"98cbd2c7\"},\n      {software_part: \"SENSEiQ board software\", version: \"SR1011 v 1.00.01\", crc_checksum: \"9E2451A0\"},\n      {software_part: \"SENSEiQ board software\", version: \"SR1011 v 1.01.03\", crc_checksum: \"ba4986fa\"},\n      {software_part: \"SENSEiQ board software\", version: \"SR1011 v 1.11.00\", crc_checksum: \"686dfc37\"}\n    ]\n  }\n]\n\nThe rowspan structure: \"LCR.iQ software part and version\" spans 5 rows. So software_part for those rows is \"LCR.iQ software part and version\". I'll normalize to \"LCR.iQ software\" or keep full text \"LCR.iQ software part and version\". Keep faithful: \"LCR.iQ software part and version\", \"I/O board software part and version\", \"SENSEiQ board software part and version\".\n\nAlso software components could be listed in components[]? The schema says components for named sub-assemblies — software parts are sub-assemblies of the calculating/indicating device (LCR.iQ software, I/O board software, SENSEiQ board). Hmm, \"SENSEiQ board\" is hardware. I think matrix_tables capture this adequately. Maybe I'll also note in the component's characteristics the validated software as string? No — matrix table suffices. Keep it clean.\n\ndocumentation folders: TC11730-1 and TC7329-5 — could go into test_reports[] as documentation_file role. The schema test_reports: id, date, pages, role. role: documentation_file. So:\n\ntest_reports: [\n  {id: \"TC11730-1\", role: \"documentation_file\"},\n  {id: \"TC7329-5\", role: \"documentation_file\"}\n]\n\nThat seems right per schema (\"Documentation folder: — TC11730-1\").\n\ncertified_type: For the overall certificate. category: \"Measuring system\" (R 117 is measuring systems for liquids other than water; the cert covers parts). The fragment only shows the two parts. certified_type.type_designations: should I list MASTERLOAD.iQ, MASTERLOADx.iQ, ESG_1, ESG_2? The certified 